      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Tomato plants, just like those pesky little cave-dwelling goblins, can be quite finicky. I'm sure it's not a lack of nutrients, but rather a case of the \"goblin's gold\" – a bit of a fungal infection, perhaps. You see, those yellowing leaves on the bottom are likely suffering from a condition called \"leaf scorch,\" which can be caused by overwatering, underwatering, or even a fungal disease like powdery mildew. It's a bit like when a group of goblins get too comfortable in their underground tunnels and start to rot from the inside out – it's a slow and insidious process.\n\nNow, let's take a closer look at your soil and watering habits. Are you providing enough nutrients for your plants, or are you overfertilizing? A bit of goblin-like cunning might come in handy here – you see, goblins are notorious for their love of shiny objects, and plants can be a bit like that too. They love a good dose of calcium and magnesium, but too much of a good thing can be, well, a bit too much.\n\nI'd recommend checking your soil pH and adjusting it if necessary. And don't",
